							<h2>CAP Data Displays</h2>
							<table><td valign="top">
							<h3><a href="/cap/profiler.jsp" class="alink">Graphical Data Display</a></h3>
							<P class="normalText">
							The real-time data display generates graphical (images).  
							Time-series displays include; Wind Speed and Direction, Spectral Moment 
							Displays (Signal Power, Signal to Noise Ratio, Radial Velocity, 
							and Velocity Variance), and RASS Temperature Displays.
							</P>
							<P class="normalText">
							The <a class="alink" href="/cap/profiler.jsp?options=full">Advanced display options</a> 
							allow user to customize display option for current and historical data, color scale, 
							units, height, time-span, background color, and image size and quality.
							</P>
							<P class="normalText">
							The maps indicating the location of profiler sites convey the status of 
							sites using color to indicate how recently data have been received from a site.
							</P>
							</td>
							<td valign="top">
							<a href="/cap/profiler.jsp">
							<img src="images/profiler_jsp.png" 
								alt="Profiler Data Display" 
								title="Profiler Data Display" 
								align="right" border=1>
							</a>
							</td></table><br>
							<table border=0 summary="">
							<tr valign="top">
								<td>
									<P class="normalText">
									<em><strong>Wind Speed and Direction</strong></em> shown in vertical profiles. 
									The barbs point in the direction the wind is blowing towards.  Color as well as the number 
									and length of the flags on each barb identify the wind speed.  Each vertical 

									column represent either one hour, 30-minute, 15-minute, 10-minute, or 5-minute averaged data, 
									depending on the resolution (or sampling period) of the radar.


									</p>
								</td>
								<td NOWRAP>
									<img src="images/capwind_t.png" 
										alt="Profiler 12 Hour Wind Plot" 
										title="Profiler 12 Hour Wind Plot">
									<img src="images/capwind168_t.png" 
										alt="Profiler 168 Hour Wind Plot" 
										title="Profiler 168 Hour Wind Plot">
								</td>
							</tr>
							</table>
							<hr>

							<table border=0 summary="">
							<tr valign="top">
								<td>
									<P class="normalText">
									<strong><em>Signal to Noise Ratio</em></strong> 
									is a measure of the amount of the radar's energy 
									that has been reflected and returned from the atmosphere, 
									expressed in decibels (dB), values can range from 20 dB (very weak, blues) 
									to 100 dB (very strong, reds).
									</p>
								</td>
								<td>
									<img src="images/SNR_t.png" 
									alt="12 Hour Signal to Noise Ratio Plot"
									title="12 Hour Signal to Noise Ratio Plot">
								</td>
							</tr>
							</table>
							<hr>


			
							<table border=0 summary="">
							<tr valign="top">
								<td>
									<P class="normalText">
									<strong><em>Signal Power</em></strong> is a measure of the amount of the radar's energy 
									that has been reflected and returned from the atmosphere. Signal Power is expressed 
									in decibels and values can range from 20 dB (very weak, blues) to 100 dB (very strong, reds).</p></td>
								<td><img src="images/sigPwr_t.png" alt="12 Hour Signal Power Plot" title="12 Hour Signal Power Plot"></td>
							</tr>
							</table>
							<hr>
			


							<table border=0 summary="">
							<tr valign="top">
								<td>
									<P class="normalText">

									<strong><em>Range Corrected Signal to Noise Ratio</em></strong> and 


                                                                        for the spherical divergence of the transmitted radar signals as they propagate away 
                                                                        from the radar antenna.  

									Once the Signal to Noise Ratio is range corrected, it is 
                                                                        directly proportional to radar reflectivity, a more widely used radar variable. 

								<td NOWRAP>

								  <img src="images/rcSNR_t.png" alt="18 Hour Range Corrected Signal Noise Level Plot" title="18 Hour Range Corrected Signal Noise Level Plot">

								  <img src="images/rcPeakPower_t.png" alt="18 Hour Range Corrected Signal Peak Power Plot" title="18 Hour Range Corrected Signal Peak Power Plot">
								</td>
							</tr>
							</table>
							<hr>
			


							<table border=0 summary="">
							<tr valign="top">
								<td>
									<P class="normalText">
									<em><strong>Radial Velocity</strong></em> is a measure of the amount of 
									Doppler Shift of the atmospheric signal returns expressed in meters per 
									second.  Referenced to the frequency of the radar (404 MHz), incoming 
									signals cause positive Doppler shifts (&gt; Tranmitted Frequency), outgoing signals 
									cause negative Doppler shifts (&lt; Transmitted Frequency).  Incoming signals are 
									colored shades of yellows and red, outgoing signals are colored shades 
									of cyan and blue.
									</p>
								</td>
								<td>
									<img src="images/radVel_t.png" 
										alt="12 Hour Radial Velocity Plot"
										title="12 Hour Radial Velocity Plot">
								</td>
							</tr>
							</table>
							<hr>

							<table border=0 summary="">
							<tr valign="top">
								<td>
									<P class="normalText">
									<em><strong>Velocity Variance</strong></em> is a measure of the spectral 
									width of atmospheric signal returns. Velocity Variance is expressed in 
									(meters/second)<sup>2</sup>.</p>  </td>
								<td><img src="images/velVar_t.png" alt="12 Hour Velocity Variance Plot" title="12 Hour Velocity Variance Plot"></td>
							</tr>
							</table>
							<hr>


							

							<table border=0 summary="">
							<tr valign="top">
								<td>
									<P class="normalText">
									<em><strong>RASS Temperature</strong></em> is a vertical profile of 
									atmospheric temperatures at each range gate of the radar. 
									Colder temperatures are shades of blue, warm temperatures are shades of red.</p></td>
								<td>
									<img src="images/rass_t.png" 
										alt="RASS Temperature Profile Plot"
										title="RASS Temperature Profile Plot">
								</td>
							</tr>
							</table>
							<hr>

							<h3><a href="/cap/fslformat.html" class="alink">Text-Based Data Display</a></h3>
